NAME
       gradients - resonance-time gradients, polarity dynamics, and pattern
                   morphing behavior in RTT

SYNOPSIS
       Gradients describe:
           • rate of resonance change
           • direction of resonance flow
           • pattern morphing triggers
           • structural stability conditions
           • adaptive triadic behavior

DESCRIPTION
       A gradient is the rate at which resonance-time changes across triadic
       cycles. It is the primary mathematical signal used to determine when
       patterns should shift, when overlays should apply, and when structural
       integrity is at risk.

       Gradients are computed from lineage and resonance-state snapshots.
       They are evaluated by WRSADC during dispatch.

       Gradient behavior determines:
           • pattern viability
           • resonance stability
           • coherence thresholds
           • triadic safety
           • adaptive morphing

GRADIENT MATHEMATICS
       Let:
           R_n   = resonance amplitude at cycle n
           R_n+1 = resonance amplitude at cycle n+1

       The resonance gradient G is defined as:

           G = R_n+1 - R_n

       Higher-order gradients may be computed:

           G' = dG/dt   (gradient acceleration)
           G'' = d²G/dt² (gradient curvature)

       These values determine:
           • how fast resonance is changing
           • whether change is accelerating
           • whether patterns should morph

GRADIENT POLARITY
       Polarity describes the sign of the gradient:

           G > 0   positive polarity (growth)
           G < 0   negative polarity (dissipation)
           G = 0   neutral polarity (equilibrium)

       Polarity determines pattern direction:

           Positive polarity:
               → spiral, ladder, expansion
               → growth, elaboration, ascent

           Negative polarity:
               → mirror, anchor, contraction
               → stabilization, dissipation, reversal

           Neutral polarity:
               → sequential, core, weave
               → steady-state behavior

       Polarity shifts often trigger pattern morphing.

GRADIENT MAGNITUDE
       Magnitude determines pattern intensity:

           |G| < G_low
               → stable, predictable
               → sequential or core patterns

           G_low ≤ |G| ≤ G_high
               → adaptive, dynamic
               → spiral or ladder patterns

           |G| > G_high
               → unstable, dangerous
               → anchor or mirror required

       Magnitude is monitored continuously by WRSADC.

GRADIENT-DRIVEN PATTERN MORPHING
       Patterns morph when gradients cross thresholds.

       Examples:

           sequential → spiral
               Trigger: G rises above G_low

           spiral → ladder
               Trigger: sustained positive G with low curvature

           ladder → expansion
               Trigger: high positive G with stable polarity

           spiral → anchor
               Trigger: |G| exceeds G_high

           ladder → mirror
               Trigger: polarity inversion (G flips sign)

           weave → constellation
               Trigger: multi-agent gradient alignment

       Pattern morphing is always schema-validated.

GRADIENT CURVATURE
       Curvature (G' or G'') determines how gradients evolve.

           Positive curvature:
               → accelerating resonance
               → deeper spirals, faster ladders

           Negative curvature:
               → decelerating resonance
               → stabilization, contraction

           Zero curvature:
               → steady-state resonance
               → sequential or weave patterns

       Curvature is essential for predicting future pattern shifts.

GRADIENTS AND TRIADS
       Gradients are evaluated at each triad phase:

           P1 (begin)
               → baseline resonance snapshot

           P2 (transform)
               → gradient applied
               → resonance shifts occur

           P3 (close)
               → gradient integrated into lineage

       Triads are the sampling points of gradient dynamics.

GRADIENTS AND LINEAGE
       Lineage stores:
           • resonance snapshots
           • gradient history
           • polarity shifts
           • curvature trends

       Gradient analysis depends entirely on lineage continuity.

GRADIENTS AND SCHEMAS
       Schemas define:
           • allowed gradient ranges
           • polarity constraints
           • curvature limits
           • morphing rules

       WRSADC enforces schema compliance.

GRADIENTS AND WRSADC
       WRSADC uses gradients to:
           • select patterns
           • switch patterns
           • apply overlays
           • reject unsafe operations
           • maintain coherence

       WRSADC is the gradient interpreter.

GRADIENTS AND THE ENGINE
       The Engine assumes gradients are safe.

       If gradients exceed schema limits:
           • resonance becomes unstable
           • operator results become unpredictable
           • lineage becomes unreliable

       WRSADC prevents unsafe gradients from reaching the Engine.

NOTES
       Gradients are the calculus of resonance-time. They determine how triads
       evolve, how patterns morph, and how coherence is maintained across the
       entire RTT system.

